Sybase ASE bulk copy fixes from FreeTDS master, backported to FreeTDS 1.5.17.

None of these are in a FreeTDS release yet (1.5.21 is the latest at the time of
writing); they are expected in 1.6. Remove this patch once FreeTDS is upgraded to
a release that contains them.

Each file this patch changes carries a notice saying so. Changes made on
2026-11-11 (the drizzle-sybase git history dates each one).

Upstream commits (https://github.com/FreeTDS/freetds):
  e1133a1685  [DOL BULK] Add TDS_REQ_DOL_BULK to capabilities.
  c431b958d2  [DOL BULK] Probe for datarows-lock on the table
  6f65b6e1ba  [DOL BULK] Row data packet layout
  4b0ff06729  [DOL BULK] Also support freebcp being invoked with database.owner.tablename
  c78eb67371  [DOL BULK] Perform the locktype query before the columns query - hidden dependency
  29de16d9c4  [DOL BULK] Documentation for sysstat2; warn about slow index for datarows-locked table
  556003bb85  tds: Detect data rows locking looking to capabilities
  797b63215a  bcp: (ASE) Correctly detect Datarows Locking for temporary tables
  0f34ecbd52  bcp in: (ASE) Adjust row buffer size formula to allow for datarows-locked format
  afc0b85cd8  freebcp: Implement -k (meaning -hKEEP_NULLS)            (libTDS part only)
  5703d0b96b  tds: Do not ignore conversion errors in Sybase bulk copy (libTDS part only)
  bce8b3791e  tds: Remove trailing zeroes from binary data in Sybase bulk copy (libTDS part only)

Why drizzle-sybase needs them:
  - Without the DOL_BULK capability and row format, ASE refuses a bulk copy into a
    data-only-locked (datarows) table: Msg 4845, "Client does not have the
    capability to bulk copy into the data-only locked table".
  - KEEP_NULLS (bcp_options BCPHINTS) is ASE bcp's --ignoredefaults: a NULL stays
    NULL instead of becoming the column default (bulkInsert's emptyAsNull).
  - A value that fails conversion must fail its row, not be sent as NULL/default.

Adaptation to 1.5.17: c431b958d2's own int conversion is kept (master later
switched to tds_try_get_int_col, which 1.5.17 does not have); otherwise the code
is master's final form of each hunk, with these fixes master does not have yet
(all checked against ASE 15.7):
  - datapages-locked tables are data-only locked too, and take the same DOL
    format; master only detects datarows (sysstat2 0x8000, not 0x4000), so a
    copy into a datapages table fails with Msg 4837 (bad row length).
  - With KEEP_NULLS, master skips reading the default values but still
    substitutes them, as empty values: a NULL char column with a default gets a
    blank instead of NULL. Here a column is treated as having no default.
  - A #temp table lives in the session's temporary database, which on a server
    with several (tempdb, tempdb_2, ...) is often not tempdb: master looks it up
    in tempdb..sysobjects, misses it, and sends the wrong row format (Msg 4837).
    Here its lock scheme is read with lockscheme(object_id, @@tempdbid).
  - The lock scheme probe looked the table up by bare name in sysobjects, which
    matches every owner's table of that name (owner.table also put the owner
    in front of sysobjects); it now reads lockscheme() of exactly the target
    object, in its database. A failed probe, or an unknown lock scheme, fails
    the copy instead of being taken for an allpages table (the probe's own
    statement errors are checked too).
  - The row buffer was sized by a formula that did not bound what the row
    writer writes: in the DOL format it left out the 2-byte offset per variable
    column (100 varbinary(8) columns given 8 bytes each wrote 93 bytes past
    it), and in the allpages format it counted the adjustment table from the
    variable data alone, not from the end of the whole row (six full
    binary(248) and a full varbinary(8) wrote 3 bytes past it). It is now sized
    for the largest row of either format, whichever columns the server makes
    fixed or variable.
  - The variable-column offsets are a 256-entry array and their count one
    byte: a row with more than 255 variable columns wrote past the array. It
    now fails instead (drizzle-sybase refuses such a table before starting).
  - Reading the column defaults ignored a failed allocation, leaving a default
    size without its value (later copied from a NULL or stale buffer); and a
    numeric column's buffer was used before its allocation was checked. Both
    now fail the copy. A default of NULL is treated as no default.
  - A NULL for a NOT NULL column without a default failed the row without a
    report when no default had been read; it is now reported (SYBEBCNN) as
    for any other column, so a failure without a report is never a refused
    value (the caller can tell an out-of-memory failure from a bad row).
  - bcp_batch() ignored a failure to open the copy again after committing a
    batch: every row sent next then failed without an error, as if refused on
    its own. It now reports SYBEBULKINSERT, still returning the batch's count
    (the batch is committed).

